Constipation in palliative care
Palliative & End of Life
Infrequent hard stools, Crampy lower abdominal pain, Left lower quadrant palpable mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-017
Reason for Visit: Intolerable lower abdominal cramping and nausea preventing the patient from taking essential oral palliative medications.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, registered nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient has not had a true bowel movement in 6 days, now presenting with severe, diffuse crampy lower abdominal pain progressively worsening over continuous days, and absolute refusal of normal dietary intake or baseline oral analgesia due to severe nausea. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was required to assess for impaction, provide immediate parenteral antiemetics, and initiate a robust bowel clearance protocol prior to the morning to restore the patient's ability to take essential oral medications.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower belly is cramping terribly. I feel completely full and sick to my stomach, I just can't take those pain pills today."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a severe, highly infrequent passage explicitly of small, distinctly hard, and severely desiccated stools over the last week. This is accompanied by a continuous, unrelenting subjective sensation of a distinctly incomplete and wholly unsatisfying rectal emptying. The severe, distinctly diffuse crampy lower abdominal pain has worsened significantly over the last 48 hours. The nurse notes paradoxical, entirely uncontrolled liquid perianal leakage strongly surrounding a suspected central impaction. The patient exhibits intense, strictly painful straining with absolutely completely unproductive actively attempted defecation. Unrelenting, profound severe nausea entirely secondary to severe bowel distension has led to an absolute, express explicit refusal to consume standard normal dietary intake or medications today. There is a clear, definitive history explicitly taking continuously escalating doses of highly constipating systemic opioid analgesics.
Functional Status: Confined to bed/chair. Requires moderate assistance for toileting.
Social/Safety Concerns: Interruption of essential baseline analgesia due to nausea, risking a severe pain crisis if not resolved promptly.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 61 Kg
General: Patient appears uncomfortable, holding their lower abdomen, and actively resisting being offered oral fluids.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is visibly distended in the lower half. A hard, expressly palpable, and distinctly large fecal mass is definitively felt explicitly deep within the left lower abdominal quadrant. Percussion reveals dullness over the descending colon. Digital rectal exam reveals a firm, dry fecal mass immediately proximal to the anal verge. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the history of infrequent hard stools, active crampy lower abdominal pain, and the left lower quadrant palpable mass confirmed on abdominal examination.
Environmental Observations: Patient is close to an ensuite bathroom. Appropriate continence aids are available.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is prescribed oral docusate only; there is no stimulant laxative prescribed alongside the high-dose opioid regimen.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Constipation in palliative care - Status: Exacerbated (Impaction and Nausea)
Differentials:

Subacute bowel obstruction (malignant) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the distal nature of the palpable mass and confirmation of fecal impaction in the rectum, rather than a proximal stricture.

Diverticulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of localized peritonism, guarding, rebound tenderness, or systemic fever.

Urinary tract infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to clear, odor-free urine output and absence of dysuria; the pain is clearly colicky and bowel-related.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe exacerbation of palliative-phase constipation directly caused by an inadequate laxative regimen running concurrent to escalating opioid use. The objective findings of a palpable left lower quadrant mass, hard stool in the rectum, and severe nausea explain the complete cessation of oral intake. Because the patient is entirely refusing their essential baseline palliative medications due to this nausea, delaying assessment until standard hours would inevitably precipitate a catastrophic, systemic pain crisis and withdrawal. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to administer parenteral antiemetics to bypass the gut and authorize targeted rectal interventions to clear the mechanical blockage.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous haloperidol for the severe nausea secondary to bowel distension. Ordered and supervised the administration of a microlax enema by the nursing staff. Overhauled the regular medication chart to cease docusate and commence a combination of a macrogol and a targeted opioid-antagonist laxative (e.g., methylnaltrexone) or a robust stimulant (senna) if unavailable.
Positive Behavioral Change: When sitting on the toilet (trigger), actively place your feet on a small footstool to elevate your knees above your hips (behavior), which correctly aligns the rectum and utilizes gravity to make passing stool significantly easier (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Continued facility nursing care.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure a footstool is permanently placed in the patient's bathroom.
Follow-up: Facility GP review in 24 hours to monitor bowel output and oral intake tolerance.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops projectile vomiting, sudden and severe worsening of the abdominal pain making the stomach hard to the touch, or completely stops passing any urine, the facility staff must contact the after-hours doctor immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Constipation in the palliative setting is almost universally driven by necessary pain medications and requires aggressive daily medical management.
Clinical Course: The bowel will not regulate itself without strong daily medications while on opioids. The new regimen must be taken strictly every day.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Nursing staff: Ensure the patient attempts to mobilize gently around the room if tolerated. Withhold oral pain medications until the nausea has settled from the injection, then reattempt.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain medications have put your bowels to sleep. The injection under your skin will stop the sickness feeling immediately so you can start taking your pills again. The enema will clear the immediate blockage, and the new daily bowel pills will wake the bowels up moving forward.
Actionable Steps: Use the footstool in the bathroom. Drink small, frequent sips of warm fluids like tea or prune juice to help soften the stool.

